# One or two sentences of genuinely local context per city.
CITY_LOCAL = {
 "Miami": "Miami-Dade County sits in Florida's High-Velocity Hurricane Zone, which has some of the strictest roofing rules in the country. Roofing products used here generally need specific local product approval, and roofs are inspected at several stages during installation. The rules were tightened after Hurricane Andrew in 1992.",
 "Tampa": "The Tampa Bay area hadn't taken many direct hurricane hits for decades until recent storms, including Hurricanes Helene and Milton in 2024, which damaged roofs across the region. Many Tampa homes are older ranch houses with shingle roofs, and Florida insurers pay close attention to roof age.",
 "Orlando": "Central Florida gets frequent summer thunderstorms and lightning, and hurricanes that cross the state can still bring damaging winds inland, as Hurricane Ian did in 2022. Asphalt shingles and concrete tile are both common on Orlando-area homes.",
 "Jacksonville": "Jacksonville covers a very large area, from beach neighborhoods exposed to salt air and nor'easters to inland suburbs. Coastal homes see faster corrosion of metal flashing and fasteners, so ask roofers about corrosion-resistant materials near the ocean.",
 "Fort Lauderdale": "Broward County, like Miami-Dade, is in Florida's High-Velocity Hurricane Zone, so roofing products and installation must meet stricter wind standards and pass multiple inspections. Concrete tile is very common on homes here.",
 "Houston": "Houston gets hurricanes and tropical storms from the Gulf, like Hurricane Harvey in 2017 and Hurricane Beryl in 2024, plus spring hail and severe thunderstorms. Heat and humidity also wear shingles out faster than in drier climates.",
 "Dallas": "The Dallas-Fort Worth area is one of the most hail-prone metro areas in the country, and big spring hailstorms regularly lead to waves of roof claims. Impact-resistant shingles are a popular upgrade here, and some Texas insurers offer discounts for them.",
 "San Antonio": "San Antonio gets intense spring hailstorms and long, hot summers. Heat and UV take a toll on shingles, and hail can damage roofs without causing an immediate leak.",
 "Austin": "Austin sees spring hail and severe storms, and also occasional winter ice storms, such as the February 2021 freeze and the 2023 ice storm that brought down trees across the city. Fallen limbs are a common cause of roof damage here.",
 "Fort Worth": "Fort Worth shares the Dallas area's heavy hail risk, with major hailstorms hitting Tarrant County in many recent years. If a storm passes through your neighborhood, an inspection can catch hail bruising before it turns into a leak.",
 "Atlanta": "Atlanta's tree cover is a big factor for roofs. Falling limbs during thunderstorms and the occasional tropical remnant cause a lot of damage, and shaded roofs are more likely to grow moss and algae. Algae-resistant shingles are common here.",
 "Charlotte": "Charlotte gets summer thunderstorms, occasional hail, and winter ice storms that can bring down trees and power lines. Remnants of hurricanes moving inland, like Helene in 2024, can also bring damaging wind and rain to the region.",
 "Raleigh": "The Raleigh area is far enough inland to avoid direct hurricane landfalls, but storms like Hurricane Fran in 1996 have still caused major tree and roof damage here. Ice storms in winter are another common cause of roof repairs.",
 "Phoenix": "Phoenix summers regularly top 110 degrees, and that heat and UV dry out shingles and the underlayment beneath tile roofs. Monsoon storms from roughly June through September bring sudden wind, dust and heavy rain. Tile and foam flat roofs are both common in the Valley.",
 "Tucson": "Tucson's monsoon season brings powerful summer storms, and its strong sun is hard on roofs. Many Tucson homes have flat or low-slope roofs with parapet walls, which need regular coating and good drainage to prevent leaks.",
 "Denver": "Denver sits in one of the most active hail zones in the country, and the Front Range sees damaging hailstorms most summers. Strong sun at high altitude and big temperature swings also age roofs faster. The City of Denver licenses roofing contractors.",
 "Colorado Springs": "Colorado Springs gets frequent summer hail and high winds coming off the mountains. Wind can lift and tear shingles, so ask roofers about wind ratings and how shingles are fastened. The Pikes Peak Regional Building Department handles roofing permits and contractor licensing here.",
 "Oklahoma City": "Oklahoma City is in the heart of Tornado Alley and also gets frequent large hail. Spring storm season is when most roof damage happens, and impact-resistant shingles are a common upgrade.",
 "Tulsa": "Tulsa gets regular spring hail, severe thunderstorms and occasional tornadoes, and winter ice storms can bring down trees. After a big storm, out-of-town crews often arrive, so check the Oklahoma registration of any roofer.",
 "Nashville": "Nashville has seen serious tornado damage in recent years, including the March 2020 tornado that crossed the city, and it gets frequent severe thunderstorms. Many Nashville homes have asphalt shingle roofs that face both storms and summer heat.",
 "Chicago": "Chicago winters bring heavy snow, ice dams and freeze-thaw cycles, and summer storms can bring hail and strong winds. Chicago has many flat-roofed two-flats and older brick homes, so flat roof systems like modified bitumen and TPO are common here. The city requires permits and licensed contractors for roofing work.",
 "Columbus": "Columbus gets a mix of everything: snow and ice in winter, and severe thunderstorms with hail and wind in spring and summer. Ice and water shield along the eaves and good attic ventilation help prevent ice dams.",
 "Cleveland": "Cleveland sits on Lake Erie and gets lake-effect snow, which means heavy snow loads and ice dams on many roofs. Older homes in the area often have multiple layers of shingles, which usually need to be torn off during a replacement.",
 "Detroit": "Detroit winters bring snow, ice dams and freeze-thaw cycles that crack flashing and sealants. Many Detroit-area homes are older and may have aging decking under the shingles, so ask how decking replacement is priced.",
 "Philadelphia": "Philadelphia has many rowhomes with flat or low-slope roofs, which commonly use rubber (EPDM), modified bitumen or coated roofs. Shared walls and roof lines mean flashing between neighboring roofs matters a lot.",
 "Pittsburgh": "Pittsburgh's hills, older housing stock and steep roofs make roofing work more complex, and winter snow and ice add wear. Steep pitches and multiple roof levels can raise the cost of a replacement.",
 "Buffalo": "Buffalo is known for lake-effect snow, and heavy snow loads and ice dams are major roof concerns. Good insulation, ventilation and ice and water shield along the eaves are especially important here.",
 "Los Angeles": "Los Angeles roofs face strong sun, long dry seasons and wildfire risk in many hillside and canyon areas, as the January 2025 fires showed. Clay and concrete tile are common, and fire-rated (Class A) roofing is required in many high fire hazard zones.",
 "San Diego": "San Diego's mild climate is easy on roofs, but sun, salt air near the coast and wildfire risk in inland and canyon areas still matter. Tile roofs are common, and their underlayment usually wears out before the tiles do.",
 "Sacramento": "Sacramento has hot, dry summers and wet winters, and the region is surrounded by wildfire-prone areas. Fire-rated roofing and ember-resistant vents are worth asking about, along with good attic ventilation for the summer heat.",
 "Seattle": "Seattle's long rainy season and mild temperatures make moss one of the biggest roof problems here. Moss holds moisture and lifts shingles, so regular cleaning, moss-resistant shingles and good flashing around chimneys and skylights all help.",
 "Las Vegas": "Las Vegas has extreme summer heat and intense UV, which break down roofing materials quickly. Concrete tile and foam or coated flat roofs are common, and monsoon storms in late summer can bring sudden wind and heavy rain.",
 "Charleston": "Charleston is a low-lying coastal city exposed to hurricanes, tropical storms and salt air, and Hurricane Hugo in 1989 caused severe damage across the area. Many historic downtown homes have metal or slate roofs that need specialized repair.",
 "New Orleans": "New Orleans faces hurricanes, heavy rain and high humidity. Hurricane Katrina in 2005 and Hurricane Ida in 2021 both caused widespread roof damage across the area. Wind-rated roofing and sealed roof decks are worth discussing with any roofer here.",
 "Minneapolis": "Minneapolis winters are long and cold, with heavy snow and frequent ice dams, and the Twin Cities also get damaging summer hail. Minnesota requires residential roofers to be licensed by the state.",
 "Kansas City": "Kansas City gets frequent spring hail and severe storms, plus winter ice and snow. Hail damage is one of the most common roof insurance claims in the area.",
 "St. Louis": "St. Louis has hot, humid summers, winter ice and snow, and spring storms with hail and occasional tornadoes. Many older brick homes in the area have steep roofs or flat sections that need careful flashing.",
 "Fort Myers": "Fort Myers and the rest of Lee County took the brunt of Hurricane Ian in 2022, one of the costliest storms in Florida history, and many roofs in the area were damaged or replaced afterward. Wind-rated materials and careful permit work matter here.",
 "West Palm Beach": "Palm Beach County sits just north of Florida's High-Velocity Hurricane Zone but still has strict wind-load requirements. Hurricanes Frances and Jeanne in 2004 damaged many roofs across the county.",
 "El Paso": "El Paso has a high-desert climate with intense sun, little rain and summer monsoon storms. Many homes have flat or low-slope roofs with parapets, which rely on good coatings and drainage.",
 "Memphis": "Memphis gets severe thunderstorms with powerful straight-line winds, like the July 2003 windstorm locals call Hurricane Elvis, which damaged roofs and trees across the city. Heat and humidity also shorten shingle life.",
 "Cincinnati": "Cincinnati's hilly neighborhoods and older homes often mean steep roofs, and the area gets winter ice and snow plus spring storms. Ice and water shield along the eaves helps prevent ice-dam leaks.",
 "Grand Rapids": "Grand Rapids gets lake-effect snow off Lake Michigan, so heavy snow loads and ice dams are common winter roof problems. Good attic ventilation and insulation help keep ice dams from forming.",
 "Rochester": "Rochester gets heavy lake-effect snow off Lake Ontario, and the area remembers the March 1991 ice storm that brought down trees across the region. Snow load and ice dams are the main roof concerns here.",
 "Fresno": "Fresno has very hot, dry summers and little rain, so heat and UV are the biggest threats to roofs. Tile and light-colored or reflective roofing help keep homes cooler.",
 "Spokane": "Unlike western Washington, Spokane has cold, snowy winters and dry summers. The area has been hit by major windstorms and ice storms, including Ice Storm 1996 and the November 2015 windstorm, both of which brought down trees onto roofs.",
 "Reno": "Reno's high-desert climate brings strong sun, dry air, winter snow and wildfire risk in surrounding areas. Big temperature swings between day and night put extra stress on roofing materials.",
 "Baton Rouge": "Baton Rouge is inland but still takes hurricane winds and heavy rain, as Hurricane Gustav showed in 2008. Humid summers and frequent thunderstorms also wear roofs down.",
 "Savannah": "Savannah is a coastal city exposed to hurricanes, tropical storms and salt air. Its historic district has many older homes with metal and slate roofs, which need roofers experienced with those materials and historic review rules.",
 "Columbia": "Columbia is known for hot, humid summers, and it gets frequent thunderstorms and the occasional inland tropical storm. Heat and humidity speed up shingle aging and algae growth.",
 "Mesa": "Mesa shares the Phoenix area's extreme summer heat and monsoon storms. Tile roofs and foam flat roofs are common, and tile roof underlayment often needs replacing before the tiles do.",
 "St. Paul": "St. Paul has long, snowy winters with frequent ice dams, and many older homes with steep roofs. The Twin Cities also get summer hailstorms, and Minnesota requires residential roofers to be state licensed.",
}

